---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { BASE } from '../../paths';
---
<Base title="Delivery Framework Cheat Sheet" track="sd">
  <h1>⚡ The Delivery Framework</h1>
  <p class="lede">Every SD answer runs this spine. The steps never change, only the content does. The clock below is the real one: a typical round loses a few minutes to introductions and keeps a few for your questions, so a 45-minute round has about 38 minutes of design. Full story and drills: <a href={`${BASE}sd/lessons/delivery-framework/`}>the Delivery Framework lesson</a>.</p>

  <div class="clock">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>45-min round</th><th>60-min round</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Intro (theirs)</td><td>3</td><td>5</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1. Requirements</td><td>6</td><td>7</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2. Core entities</td><td>2</td><td>3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3. API</td><td>4</td><td>5</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4. High-level design</td><td>10</td><td>12</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5. Deep dives</td><td><strong>13</strong></td><td><strong>20</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>6. Wrap-up</td><td>3</td><td>3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Your questions (theirs)</td><td>4</td><td>5</td></tr>
        <tr class="total"><td>Total</td><td>45</td><td>60</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="checkpoints"><strong>Four checkpoints to write in the corner of the board:</strong> 45-min round <code>9 · 15 · 25 · 38</code> (leaving requirements · starting to draw · starting deep dives · wrapping up). 60-min round <code>12 · 20 · 32 · 52</code>.</p>
    <p class="checkpoints">If the interviewer really gives you the full 45 minutes of design, the same spine on a clean clock is: requirements 0–6, entities and API 6–12, high-level 12–25, deep dives 25–42, wrap-up 42–45. Proportions are the point: <strong>a third or more of design time goes to deep dives; requirements get a sixth or less.</strong></p>
  </div>

  <div class="phase" data-min="≈ 6 min">
    <h3>1. Requirements</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Functional:</strong> "Users can…" — pick the 2–3 core flows, explicitly park the rest ("out of scope: analytics, admin").</li>
      <li><strong>Non-functional, with numbers:</strong> scale (DAU, read:write ratio), latency target, consistency vs availability, durability. These few numbers are the estimation — they belong <em>inside</em> requirements.</li>
      <li>At most five clarifying questions, each one that would change a box. When the answer is "up to you": assume, say it, write it down, move on.</li>
      <li>Bridge sentence at minute 9, felt finished or not: "Good — here are the core entities."</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="phase" data-min="≈ 2 min">
    <h3>2. Core entities</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The nouns on the board, with the relationships. Call out the M:N joins explicitly — say the join table out loud (your known trap).</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="phase" data-min="≈ 4 min">
    <h3>3. API</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Core endpoints only — method, path, request/response shape. REST unless the problem screams otherwise. Cursor pagination for lists; idempotency keys for anything that creates or charges.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="phase" data-min="≈ 10 min · starts 0:15">
    <h3>4. High-Level Design</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Draw: client → LB/gateway → services → data stores. Trace ONE core flow end to end through the boxes, one requirement at a time.</li>
      <li>Every box earns its place — say why it exists in one sentence.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="phase" data-min="≈ 13 min · starts 0:25">
    <h3>5. Deep Dives</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>2–3 hard parts. Lead with the one the interviewer poked at. This is where deeper arithmetic happens — compute a number when it is about to decide something, and only then.</li>
      <li>Format per dive: problem → 2 options → tradeoff → <strong>commit to one</strong>. No hand-waves: cache means key shape + TTL + invalidation + failure plan, or it doesn't count.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="phase" data-min="≈ 3 min · starts 0:38">
    <h3>6. Wrap-Up</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Bottlenecks you'd attack next, what you'd monitor, what breaks at 10×.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <blockquote>
    <p><strong>Your personal red flags</strong> (from real mock data): ① answering a different layer than asked (identity ≠ tenant ≠ role ≠ resource), ② "cache it in Redis" without key/TTL/invalidation, ③ going silent instead of reasoning out loud, ④ skipping the estimation → design bridge sentence.</p>
  </blockquote>

  <style>
    .lede { color: var(--text-dim); }
    .clock { border: 1px solid var(--accent-dim); border-radius: var(--radius); background: var(--bg-raised); padding: .6rem 1.2rem 1rem; margin: 1rem 0; }
    .clock table { width: 100%; font-size: .9rem; }
    .clock td:not(:first-child), .clock th:not(:first-child) { text-align: center; font-family: var(--mono); }
    .clock .total td { border-top: 2px solid var(--border); font-weight: 700; }
    .checkpoints { font-size: .88rem; color: var(--text-dim); }
    .phase {
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      background: var(--bg-raised);
      padding: 0.4rem 1.2rem 0.7rem;
      margin: 0.8rem 0;
      position: relative;
    }
    .phase::after {
      content: attr(data-min);
      position: absolute;
      top: 0.85rem;
      right: 1.1rem;
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      color: var(--accent);
    }
    .phase h3 { margin: 0.6rem 0 0.3rem; }
  </style>
</Base>
